∈{1,2,3} under the GPT-2 tokenizer, majority-class next-token) are evaluated on EM and CharES only; NLL is left as "—" for these baselines since they lack a probabilistic foundation.", "Hardware requirement is a single external GPU box with ≥24 GB VRAM (or a 16 GB card with int8/int4 quantization); no CPU/in-bubble fallback paths are written for CodeLlama-7B."], "uncertainties": ["Exact HF commit SHAs for `gpt2`, `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf`, and `RepoBench/RepoBench` at execution time are not known in advance; the design instructs the operator to pin them at download and record in `run_config.yaml`.", "The exact RepoBench `cross_file_first` field schema names (e.g., whether the intra-file source is `current_file_content` or `file_context`) are documented from public spec but should be reconciled against the official loader at execution time; deviations are controlled but not pre-listed.", "Whether CodeLlama-7B base ships with the `<PRE>/<SUF>/<MID>` sentinels already registered in its tokenizer is verified at load time and re-added if missing; this is a single defensive step rather than a redesign.", "The realized N (Python-filtered test-set size) is reported at start-of-run; the design's power claim ≥ 0.8 holds for N ≥ 500; below that the run is flagged underpowered but is not invalidated.", "Whether the researcher's external machine has 24 GB+ VRAM or only 16 GB; the int8/int4 quantization fallback is described but the throughput/time budget is not pre-quantified.", "Data contamination between CodeLlama's pre-training crawl and RepoBench source projects is acknowledged once and a sensitivity filter is sketched, but is not built into the headline procedure.", "The prompt-format asymmetry (plain concat vs. PSM FIM) is acknowledged once and not probed empirically within this design.", "Tokenizer asymmetry is acknowledged once: NLL is reported per-model only; no cross-model NLL comparison is claimed.", "The majority-class and n-gram baselines use the GPT-2 tokenizer; whether to additionally report them under CodeLlama's tokenizer is open but left out of scope for execution.", "The decision to omit per-example fixed-effect adjustments (e.g., conditioning on gold-token length) is a default; if effect heterogeneity is hypothesised, this is a follow-up analysis, not a primary result."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-7eb6a2a6-9aab-4c36-949e-e04b51623e45", "tool_call": {"id": "call-7eb6a2a6-9aab-4c36-949e-e04b51623e45", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"summary": "Deliver full experiment design", "stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design \u2014 GPT-2 small vs. CodeLlama-7B base on RepoBench Python `cross_file_first`\n\n## Preamble & Constraints\n\nThis document is a **portable, execution-ready design** for an external GPU-equipped machine. The current sandbox (bubblewrap, no GPU, no network, no ML dependencies) **cannot execute it**; that constraint is acknowledged here and is not revisited elsewhere in this design. Execution prerequisites:\n\n- 1\u00d7 GPU with \u226524 GB VRAM for `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf` in fp16 (80 GB+ A100 or 24 GB cards with int8/int4 quantization via `bitsandbytes`); or an int4-quantized 7B fit on a 16 GB card with measurable but acceptable throughput loss.\n- \u2265100 GB free disk for model snapshots + dataset cache.\n- Internet access (or staged local copies of): Hugging Face dataset `RepoBench/RepoBench`, model IDs `gpt2` and `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf`.\n\nPinned runtime (single version per package; deviation must be reported):\n\n| Package | Version |\n| --- | --- |\n| Python | 3.11.x |\n| `torch` | 2.4.1 |\n| `transformers` | 4.45.2 |\n| `tokenizers` | 0.20.0 |\n| `datasets` | 3.2.0 |\n| `huggingface_hub` | 0.25.2 |\n| `accelerate` | 1.0.1 |\n| `safetensors` | 0.4.5 |\n| `python-Levenshtein` | 0.25.1 |\n| `scipy` | 1.13.1 |\n| `numpy` | 1.26.4 |\n\nModel checkpoints are pinned at download time by `revision=` (Hugging Face commit SHA recorded in the run log). Recommended snapshot revisions at execution time:\n\n- `gpt2` \u2014 pin to the HF `gpt2` model card revision present at execution time; OpenAI GPT-2 small (124M).\n- `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf` \u2014 pin to the latest non-`Instruct`, non-`-Python` revision present at execution time.\n\nDataset:\n\n- `datasets.load_dataset(\"RepoBench/RepoBench\", \"cross_file_first\")` \u2014 the `cross_file_first` configuration, **Python subset only** (filter `language == \"python\"` over each split).\n\n---\n\n## Research Question & Hypotheses\n\n**RQ.** Controlling for context budget (1,024 tokens), how does GPT-2 small (124M, general) compare to CodeLlama-7B base (7B, code-specialized) on line-level completion quality over RepoBench Python `cross_file_first`?\n\n**H1 (direction).** `CodeLlama-7B base > GPT-2 small` on (a) Exact Match and (b) character-level Edit Similarity, paired by example. Pre-registered exits: H1 fails only if the 95% CI on the paired difference excludes 0 in the wrong direction.\n\n**H2 (likelihood).** Per-example per-line macro negative log-likelihood (`NLL`) is lower for CodeLlama-7B base than for GPT-2 small over the same gold continuations. Direction pre-registered: `NLL(CodeLlama) < NLL(GPT-2)`.\n\nPre-registered hypothesis test for each metric: bootstrap 95% CI on the **per-example paired difference** (CodeLlama \u2212 GPT-2), B = 1,000 resamples,resampler seed = 0. McNemar's test on EM; Wilcoxon signed-rank on ES and NLL. **No** multiple-comparisons correction \u2014 the three metrics are correlated by construction (same predictions), so Holm/Bonferroni is noted-as-overly-conservative here. Uncorrected p-values reported.\n\n---\n\n## Dataset & Splits\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\n\nds = load_dataset(\"RepoBench/RepoBench\", \"cross_file_first\", revision=\"<pinned>\")\ntest_py = ds[\"test\"].filter(lambda r: r[\"language\"] == \"python\")\n```\n\n- **Primary eval split:** full `test` set of the Python-filtered `cross_file_first` subset. No re-splitting. If N falls below 500 after filtering, document the realized N, do not sub-sample further.\n- **N reporting:** print `len(test_py)` at the start of the run; the run is invalid if N < 250 (paired-test power floor \u2014 see Threats to Validity).\n\n### Field-to-prompt assembly (locked)\n\nEach example carries (canonical schema; verified against the official loader at execution time, deviations controlled):\n\n| Field | Used for | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `context` (list of dicts `{file_name, import_name, code}`) | cross-file block | ordered by `import_name` appearance order in the target file |\n| `file_name`, `current_file_content` (or `file_context`) | intra-file prefix/suffix source | prefix = code before the masked `next_line`; suffix = code after |\n| `next_line` (str) | gold target | the first masked line to predict |\n| `language` | filter | `== \"python\"` |\n\n**Locked assembly rule (single source of truth \u2014 pseudocode):**\n\n```\ndef assemble(example):\n    cross_file = [f[\"code\"] for f in example[\"context\"]]\n    prefix     = intra_prefix(example)\n    suffix     = intra_suffix(example)\n    gold       = example[\"next_line\"].strip()\n    return cross_file, prefix, suffix, gold\n```\n\nBoth models consume the same `cross_file`, `prefix`, `suffix`, `gold`. The 1,024-token budget is enforced **once** on the GPT-2 tokenizer's token sequence, then mirrored (same byte/char-spans) on the CodeLlama tokenizer.\n\n---\n\n## Models & Pins\n\n| Row | HF ID | Params (M) | dtype (eval) | Quantization (fallback) | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Primary small general LM | `gpt2` | 124 | fp32 | \u2013 | **Primary** |\n| Primary larger code-specialized LM | `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf` | 6,740 | fp16 | int8 (bnb) if VRAM < 24 GB | **Primary** |\n| Optional robustness (appendix only) | `gpt2-medium` | 355 | fp32 | \u2013 | Appendix row, **not** in main table |\n\nNotes:\n- Parameter counts: GPT-2 small \u2248 124M; CodeLlama-7B \u2248 6.74B (reported as 7B by Meta).\n- GPT-2 is loaded with `pad_token_id = tokenizer.eos_token_id`.\n- CodeLlama is loaded with `torch_dtype=torch.float16`, `device_map=\"auto\"` (or `\"cuda:0\"`), and the PSM sentinel special tokens `<PRE>`, `<SUF>`, `<MID>` are registered (they ship with the tokenizer; verified at load time and re-added if missing).\n- `model.eval()` and `torch.no_grad()` for all forward passes.\n\n---\n\n## Prompt Assembly\n\n### Retrieval/excerpt policy (identical for both models)\n\n1. Build `cross_file_blocks: List[str]` by iterating `example[\"context\"]` in `import_name` order; for each file, set `block = f\"# FILE: {file_name}\\n{code}\\n\\n\"`.\n2. Build `intra_prefix`, `intra_suffix`, `gold = example[\"next_line\"].rstrip(\"\\n\")`.\n3. Tokenize a budget-watching skeleton with the GPT-2 tokenizer to estimate token counts per segment.\n4. Greedily assemble, in priority order: **(a) full prefix**, **(b) cross-file blocks in order**, **(c) suffix** (CodeLlama only). Truncation within a segment keeps the **trailing** slice (most recent cross-file block first; prefix kept from the tail if room is exhausted).\n5. Stop when the concatenated token count exceeds **1,024 \u2212 `max_new_tokens`** (= 960). The 64-token headroom is the same for both models.\n6. The exact truncation plan is recorded per example in the run log for audit.\n\n### Template A \u2014 GPT-2 small (plain concat, no FIM)\n\n```\n# FILE: <rel_path_1>\n<code_1>\n\n# FILE: <rel_path_2>\n<code_2>\n... (ordered by import appearance)\n\n# PREFIX:\n<intra-file code before the masked line>\n\n# COMPLETE:\n```\n\nThe model continues after `# COMPLETE:`.\n\n### Template B \u2014 CodeLlama-7B (PSM FIM, sentinels `<PRE>`/`<SUF>`/`<MID>`)\n\n```\n<PRE># FILE: <rel_path_1>\n<code_1>\n\n# FILE: <rel_path_2>\n<code_2>\n... (ordered by import appearance)\n\n# PREFIX:\n<intra-prefix code><SUF><intra-suffix code><MID>\n```\n\nThe model continues after `<MID>`. The post-MID generation is post-processed identically to the GPT-2 branch (see Decoding).\n\n### Prompt-format asymmetry \u2014 explicit note\n\nThe asymmetry (sentinel FIM for CodeLlama vs. plain concat for GPT-2) is **deliberate**: each model is evaluated in its strongest documented native format for line completion. It is recorded here once, reported once in Threats to Validity, and not adjusted for post hoc.\n\n---\n\n## Decoding & Generation Settings\n\n| Hyperparameter | Value | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `do_sample` | `False` | greedy |\n| `num_beams` | `1` | greedy |\n| `temperature` | `1.0` | unused under greedy but pinned for reproducibility |\n| `top_k`, `top_p` | \u2014 | n/a under greedy |\n| `max_new_tokens` | `64` | identical for both models |\n| `min_new_tokens` | `1` | identical for both models |\n| `eos_token_id`, `pad_token_id` | model's tokenizer | `pad_token_id = eos_token_id` |\n| Stop / truncate rule | first `\\n` in the generated continuation \u2192 truncate to first line and treat the first non-empty line as the prediction | applied identically to both models |\n| Determinism | same `torch` seed, `numpy` seed = 0; only one generation per (example, model) | greedy is seed-invariant for weights; tokenization corners are seed-checked |\n\n---\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n\n### Primary metrics (three, fixed)\n\n1. **Exact Match (EM), top-1, first non-empty line.** Define `pred = first_non_empty_line(generation).strip()`; `gold = example[\"next_line\"].strip()`. `EM = 1 if pred == gold else 0`. Compared at the level: per-example binary. **Aggregator: mean over test set.**\n2. **Character-level Edit Similarity (CharES).** `CharES = 1 \u2212 Levenshtein.distance(pred, gold) / max(len(pred), len(gold))`. Implemented as `python-Levenshtein`'s `ratio()`. Same `pred`, `gold` definitions as EM. **Aggregator: mean over test set.**\n3. **Per-line macro negative log-likelihood (NLL).** For each example, teacher-force the gold tokens one at a time given the **prompt only** (excluding `<MID>` token from the loss). Per-example `NLL = \u2212 (1/T) \u00b7 \u03a3_{i=1..T} log p_\u03b8(gold_i | prompt, gold_<i)`, where `T = len(tokenize(gold, model_tokenizer))`. Aggregator: **macro-mean over test set** (each example contributes equally, regardless of gold-token length). NLL is reported **per model**, since cross-tokenizer comparability is broken by tokenizer asymmetry.\n\n### Tokenizers in metric code (locked)\n\n- EM uses `str.strip()`; tokenizer-agnostic.\n- CharES uses Python `str` characters; tokenizer-agnostic.\n- NLL uses each model's native tokenizer; results are intra-model summaries only.\n\n### Paired statistical comparison\n\n- **Unit of pairing:** identical example index per (CodeLlama, GPT-2). For every i in 0..N-1, compute d_i = metric_i(CodeLlama) \u2212 metric_i(GPT-2).\n- EM, ES, NLL each produce a paired-difference vector d \u2208 \u211d^N (resp. \u211d^N for ES / NLL).\n- **Bootstrap 95% CI on the paired mean:** B = 1,000 resamples of the d vector (with replacement), seed = 0, percentile method. Output: `[lo, hi]`.\n- **Significance:**\n  - **McNemar's test** on EM (CodeLlama wins / GPT-2 wins / tie counts).\n  - **Wilcoxon signed-rank** (`scipy.stats.wilcoxon`, zero-method=\"wilcox\", alternative=\"two-sided\") on ES and NLL.\n- **Effect sizes (reported, not pre-registered gates):** Cohen's d for ES and NLL (paired); odds ratio for EM.\n- **No multiple-comparisons correction.** The three primary metrics are correlated by construction (they consume the same predictions), so Holm/Bonferroni would be overly conservative for this paired setup. Uncorrected p-values are reported transparently.\n\n### Output files per run\n\n- `predictions_gpt2.jsonl` \u2014 `{example_id, pred, gold, em, char_es, nll}` per line.\n- `predictions_codellama7b.jsonl` \u2014 same schema.\n- `metrics.json` \u2014 the result table (template below).\n- `truncation_log.jsonl` \u2014 per-example truncation record.\n- `run_config.yaml` \u2014 versions, seeds, HF revisions.\n\n---\n\n## Baselines\n\nFour trivial baselines, each producing a `pred` per example and evaluated against EM and CharES (NLL is not applicable to the deterministic copy baselines; reported as `\u2014` for those rows).\n\n1. **Copy-last-line.** `pred = last_non_empty_line(prefix).strip()`.\n2. **Copy-nearest-imported-symbol.** Identify the most recent `import` (or `from \u2026 import`) in `prefix`; locate the imported symbol's definition in the cross-file context (linear scan over blocks); `pred = first_non_empty_line(definition).strip()`. If absent, fall back to `copy-last-line`.\n3. **N-gram (n \u2208 {1, 2, 3}), GPT-2 tokenizer, corpus-built.** Pre-compute once over the test split: for n = 1, 2, 3, build a dict `ngram \u2192 Counter[next_ngram]` over the concatenation of all `prefix + gold`-tokenized sequences. At evaluation, look up the last n prefix tokens; take the most-frequent continuation; decode to text; `pred = first_non_empty_line(continuation).strip()`. If the last n-gram is unseen, back off to n\u22121, then to copy-last-line.\n4. **Majority-class next-token, GPT-2 tokenizer.** `pred = decode(most_frequent_next_token_over_all_test_prefix_tokens)`. Tied tokens broken by lexical order.\n\nNLL is not reported for these baselines (no probabilistic foundation); a `\u2014` placeholder is written to the table.\n\n---\n\n## Result Table Template\n\n### Main head-to-head (3 primary metrics \u00d7 2 models + paired rows)\n\n| System | EM (%) \u2191 | CharES (%) \u2191 | NLL \u2193 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| GPT-2 small (124M) | mean (bootstrap 95% CI) | mean (bootstrap 95% CI) | mean (bootstrap 95% CI) |\n| CodeLlama-7B base | mean (bootstrap 95% CI) | mean (bootstrap 95% CI) | mean (bootstrap 95% CI) |\n| **\u0394 (CodeLlama \u2212 GPT-2)** | \u0394EM [95% CI], McNemar p, OR | \u0394ES [95% CI], Wilcoxon p, Cohen's d | \u0394NLL (lower-is-better; report sign and [95% CI]), Wilcoxon p, Cohen's d |\n\n### Baseline rows\n\n| Baseline | EM (%) \u2191 | CharES (%) \u2191 | NLL \u2193 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Copy-last-line | mean | mean | \u2014 |\n| Copy-nearest-imported-symbol | mean | mean | \u2014 |\n| N-gram n=1 (GPT-2 tok) | mean | mean | \u2014 |\n| N-gram n=2 | mean | mean | \u2014 |\n| N-gram n=3 | mean | mean | \u2014 |\n| Majority-class next-token | mean | mean | \u2014 |\n\n### Appendix (optional, NOT in main table)\n\n| System | EM (%) | CharES (%) | NLL |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| GPT-2 medium (355M) | ... | ... | ... |\n| \u0394 (CodeLlama \u2212 GPT-2 medium) | ... | ... | ... |\n\nThe appendix row is included only if the researcher opts in; it is explicitly out of scope for the main head-to-head per the design contract.\n\n---\n\n## Threats to Validity\n\n1. **Pretraining-data contamination.** CodeLlama-7B's training crawl includes large quantities of public GitHub Python that may overlap with RepoBench's source projects; no public per-example contamination audit exists for this benchmark\u2013model pair. Sensitivity (not redesign): if contamination is suspected, restrict the analysis to examples whose target project's last public commit postdates a chosen cutoff (e.g., 2023-08, the public CodeLlama cutoff). This is a single-shot caveat, not a procedural change.\n2. **Tokenizer asymmetry.** CharES and EM are tokenizer-agnostic; NLL is reported **per model only**. No cross-model NLL comparison is claimed.\n3. **Prompt-format asymmetry.** Plain concatenation for GPT-2 vs. PSM FIM `<PRE>/<SUF>/<MID>` for CodeLlama is **deliberate** (each model's strongest native line-completion format) and is **not** adjusted for post hoc. Reported once here; not probed further.\n4. **Single-seed / single-temperature evaluation.** Greedy is deterministic, but transformers generation can exhibit minor numerical drift across library versions; library versions are pinned and the run re-runs are expected to be bit-identical given identical seeds and identical weights. No seed-averaging is performed for greedy.\n5. **Paired-test power.** With N \u2265 500 and an expected single-metric effect \u2265 5 percentage points (EM) / \u2265 5 ES points, paired tests at \u03b1 = 0.05 detect the hypothesized effect with > 0.8 power. If realized N < 250, the run is flagged as underpowered and reported alongside directional CIs only.\n\n---\n\n## Reproducibility Checklist\n\n- [ ] `requirements.txt` frozen to the pinned versions in Preamble.\n- [ ] Hugging Face HF revision SHAs recorded in `run_config.yaml` for: dataset `RepoBench/RepoBench`, model `gpt2`, model `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf`.\n- [ ] `torch`, `numpy` seeds set to 0; `transformers` cache purged per run; offline inference after warm-up.\n- [ ] Hardware recorded: GPU model, VRAM, driver, CUDA.\n- [ ] Prompt-assembly `assemble(example)` function bit-identical between the two model branches and committed to the repo.\n- [ ] Truncation log (`truncation_log.jsonl`) committed: per-example, the byte-spans that survived the 960-token budget for both models.\n- [ ] All four metric implementations unit-tested on 10 handcrafted pairs and pinned to the metric definitions in Metrics.\n- [ ] Output JSONLs hashed (SHA256) and stored alongside `metrics.json`.\n- [ ] `metrics.json` schema versioned (`schema: \"v1\"`).\n- [ ] Code archive archived (git tag + tarball), with `run_config.yaml`, `predictions_*.jsonl`, `truncation_log.jsonl`, and `metrics.json`.\n- [ ] N (Python-filtered test set size) reported at the top of `metrics.json`; run invalid if N < 250.", "evidence": "Workspace inspection (`ls -la /workspace`, `find -maxdepth 3`, `cat /workspace/task.json`) confirmed only `task.json` is present; no scaffolding, datasets, or weights are staged. The task contract (\"design-only; repositories and compute are not required\") and the environment description (network=disabled, GPU=disabled, no preinstalled deps) justify declaring one sandbox-impossibility line in Preamble and not proposing in-bubble CPU fallback paths for CodeLlama-7B. External probes (`getent hosts huggingface.co` \u2192 failure; `curl https://huggingface.co` \u2192 exit 6 / Could not resolve host; `nvidia-smi` not present; `import torch` \u2192 ModuleNotFoundError) closed off execution entirely. The five researcher resolutions are encoded directly: (1) GPT-2 small is the only primary small-LM row, GPT-2 medium is appendix-only and gated by an opt-in callout; (2) CodeLlama-7B base is the only primary row, no -Python/-Instruct variants appear anywhere; (3) primary metrics are exactly EM + CharES + NLL with bootstrap-CIs on paired differences, McNemar on EM, Wilcoxon on ES and NLL, no multi-comparison correction, no token-ES, no CodeBLEU; (4) context window is 1,024 tokens only, no 4,096 row; (5) the design is portable to an external GPU machine. Carry-forward approvals (PSM FIM marker `<PRE>/<SUF>/<MID>` for CodeLlama, plain concat for GPT-2, both reported as a deliberate documented asymmetry; identical retrieval/excerpt policy; greedy beam=1 T=1.0; max_new_tokens=64; first-newline truncation; pinned library versions; full RepoBench Python test split unchanged; paired test design; four trivial baselines per the researcher's enumeration; contamination caveat stated once as a limit) are placed in the matching sections. Field-to-prompt assembly is locked to a single `assemble(example)` pseudocode and to `datasets.load_dataset('RepoBench/RepoBench','cross_file_first')` with a Python language filter, per the researcher's requested verbatim loader call.", "assumptions": ["All five researcher resolutions are taken as locked-in design inputs and not re-opened in this stage.", "GPT-2 small (124M, `gpt2`) is the sole primary small-LM row; GPT-2 medium (355M) appears only as an optional appendix row gated by an opt-in.", "CodeLlama-7B base (`codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf`) is the sole primary larger code-specialized LM row; `-Python` and `-Instruct` variants are entirely out of scope.", "The RepoBench canonical field schema (context list of {file_name, import_name, code}, prefix/suffix/next_line) is as documented in the public RepoBench loader; any deviation discovered at execution time is recorded as a controlled variant, not a redesign.", "Primary metrics are exactly three: EM (top-1, first non-empty line, .strip()-normalized), CharES via python-Levenshtein, and per-example per-line macro NLL computed under each model's native tokenizer with prompt identical to the generation prompt; token-ES and CodeBLEU are out of scope.", "Hypothesis tests are bootstrap 95% CIs on paired differences (B=1,000, seed=0), McNemar for EM, Wilcoxon signed-rank for ES and NLL; no Holm/Bonferroni across the three primary metrics.", "Context window is exactly 1,024 tokens (with 64-token max_new_tokens headroom) for both models; no 4,096-token ablation row is in scope.", "Decoding is strictly greedy (beam=1, temperature=1.0 unused, no sampling); truncation rule is \"first newline \u2192 first non-empty line of the prediction\"; same for both models.", "Library versions are pinned to a specific tuple (transformers 4.45.2, torch 2.4.1, tokenizers 0.20.0, datasets 3.2.0, etc.); Model/dataset revisions are pinned by HF commit SHA at download time.", "RepoBench Python subset is taken from the official `cross_file_first` config filtered to `language == \"python\"`; no re-splitting or sub-sampling.", "The four trivial baselines (copy-last-line, copy-nearest-imported-symbol, n-gram n\u2208{1,2,3} under the GPT-2 tokenizer, majority-class next-token) are evaluated on EM and CharES only; NLL is left as \"\u2014\" for these baselines since they lack a probabilistic foundation.", "Hardware requirement is a single external GPU box with \u226524 GB VRAM (or a 16 GB card with int8/int4 quantization); no CPU/in-bubble fallback paths are written for CodeLlama-7B."], "uncertainties": ["Exact HF commit SHAs for `gpt2`, `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-hf`, and `RepoBench/RepoBench`